course: everyday
lesson: 304
title: Outdoor games, playgrounds and the rules of sports
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Running, climbing and sliding are great fun, and a few rules stop the accidents that turn fun into a trip to hospital.
explain: Never run with a lollipop or a pencil in your mouth or a sharp thing in your hand, because if you trip it can be pushed into your throat or eye.
explain: Ropes, scarves and hoodie cords can catch on climbing frames and strangle a child, and an unanchored soccer goal can tip over and crush someone.
explain: Example: a girl in Glasgow and a girl in Chicago both say they love «football». The Scottish girl means soccer, and the American girl means American football; each sport has its own rules.
explain: Sports rules make games fair: in soccer the offside rule stops attackers waiting by the goal, and in cricket a bowler must not bend and straighten the arm like a throw.
fact: Come home when the street lights come on or at the agreed time, because drivers cannot see you well in the dark and parents worry.
fact: Never run with things in your mouth or sharp things in your hand; a fall can push them into the throat or eye. Carry a pencil or scissors pointing down, and walk.
fact: Never tie ropes to playground equipment or climb wearing a scarf or hoodie cords, because they can strangle.
fact: «Football» means soccer in the UK, Ireland and most of the world, but American football in the USA and Canada, and often Australian rules or rugby league in Australia.
fact: In soccer an attacker is offside if, when a teammate passes the ball forward, he is nearer the other goal than the ball and the second-last defender (the goalkeeper usually counts as one).
fact: Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal; tell an adult if it wobbles, because unanchored goals can fall and crush children.
fact: In cricket the bowler must bowl, not throw: the elbow must not bend and straighten as the ball is let go.
fact: Take off rings, earrings and necklaces before sport, because they can catch and tear skin.
fact: Do not try backflips without a trained coach; landing on the head can cause serious neck injury.
fact: Sled sitting up and feet first, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds; head-first sledding causes injuries.
fact: Stop sledding when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.
exercise: A boy in Ohio is playing tag with friends and the street lights come on. What should he do? | Go home, as agreed with his parents | Keep playing until he is called | Hide so nobody finds him | Move the game into the road where it is lighter
exercise: Why is playing outside after dark more dangerous? | Drivers cannot see you well and your parents worry | The air becomes poisonous at night | The air turns cold and damp at night, and that makes children ill | Street lights make it too bright to see
exercise: A girl wants to run to the swings with a lollipop in her mouth. Why is that a bad idea? | If she falls, the stick can be pushed into her throat | The lollipop will melt in the sun and make her hands and face sticky | She will not be able to breathe properly while she is running | The sugar will make her heart beat too fast while she is running
exercise: A boy ties a skipping rope to the top of the climbing frame to swing on. What is right? | Never tie ropes to equipment, because they can strangle | It is fine if he ties a strong knot | It is fine if the rope is short | It is fine as long as a grown-up friend holds tight to the other end of it
exercise: Why should you tuck away or remove hoodie cords before climbing at the playground? | They can catch on equipment and strangle you | They make you climb more slowly | They get dirty on the equipment | They tangle with other children's cords and you both get stuck there
exercise: A boy from Manchester and a boy from Texas both say «I play football». What might be going on? | The Manchester boy means soccer and the Texas boy means American football | They both mean exactly the same game | They both mean rugby with an oval ball | They both mean the same game, but the Texas boy plays it with a smaller, harder ball
exercise: In soccer, when is an attacker offside? | When a forward pass reaches him and he is nearer the goal than the ball and the second-last defender | Whenever he steps into the other team's half of the pitch before the ball, even with no pass at all | Whenever he touches the ball with his hands | Whenever he kicks the ball over the goal
exercise: A soccer goal in the park wobbles when children lean on it. What should they do? | Stop climbing on it and tell an adult | Hang from the crossbar to hold it still | Push it back and keep playing | Sit on top to weigh it down
exercise: Why are unanchored soccer goals dangerous? | They can tip over and crush a child | They make the ball bounce in odd ways | They are always too small for real games | They rust and slowly turn the net brown
exercise: A boy in Melbourne is learning to bowl in cricket. What does the rule say about his arm? | He must not bend and straighten his elbow as he lets the ball go | He must bend his arm as much as he can, so the ball spins off his fingers | He must throw the ball as hard as he can | He must keep his arm below his waist, rolling the ball along the ground like in bowling
exercise: Before a basketball game a girl is wearing hoop earrings and a ring. What should she do? | Take them off before playing | Keep them on for good luck in the match | Tape her fingers so the ring stays in | Turn the earrings round to the back
exercise: Why should you take off jewellery before sport? | It can catch and tear the skin | It makes you much slower on court | The referee may want to keep it | The metal gets too cold to wear
exercise: Your friend wants to try a backflip on the trampoline after watching a video. What is right? | Do not try flips without a trained coach | Try it if somebody films it | Try it on the edge of the trampoline | Try it first on the grass instead
exercise: How should you ride a sled down a snowy hill? | Sitting up, feet first, away from roads, trees and ponds | Lying down head first on the sled, because that way you go faster and steer better | Standing up like on a skateboard | Backwards so you can see who is behind
exercise: It is getting dark but the hill is still full of snow. What should you do? | Stop sledding because you cannot see obstacles | Sled faster to finish before it is dark | Sled only on the steepest part | Keep going on the hill until someone from home comes out and calls you in
test: A girl in London says she watched «football» last night, and her cousin in Boston says the same. What is most likely true? | They probably watched two different sports | They definitely watched the same match | Both watched rugby on television | Both watched a basketball game
test: A boy is sledding head first down a hill towards a line of trees. What is the main danger? | Hitting a tree head first and hurting his head or neck | Getting snow down the back of his coat and catching a bad cold from it | Going too slowly to reach the bottom | Making the sled scratched underneath
test: Why are flips dangerous without a coach? | You can land on your head and seriously hurt your neck | You will feel dizzy for the rest of the week | The trampoline springs might wear out | Flips are not allowed on any trampoline, and you'd be banned from the park for good
test: A player waits right next to the other goal, with only the goalkeeper in front of him, and a teammate passes the ball forward to him. What should the referee call? | Offside | A penalty | A handball | A corner kick
test: Which is the safest way to carry a pencil when hurrying across the classroom? | In your hand pointing down, walking | In your mouth so your hands are free | Tucked behind your ear while you run | Point first in your hand while running
test: A soccer goal at school has no pegs or weights holding it down. What is the right thing to do? | Tell a teacher and keep off it | Climb up to check it is still strong | Hang from the bar to test it out | Kick the post to see if it falls
test: Which is true about the cricket bowling rule? | Bending and straightening the arm to throw the ball is against the rules | Bowlers must always bend the arm hard at the elbow so the ball goes faster | Bowlers may throw the ball like a baseball | Bowlers may throw the ball overarm like a baseball pitcher, as long as they are aiming at the stumps
test: A boy in Leeds plays rugby wearing a chain necklace. What could happen? | The chain could catch and hurt his neck or skin | The chain could make the ball slip | He could lose points for wearing it | The chain would bring him good luck
test: Why is a scarf dangerous on a playground slide? | It can catch on the slide and pull tight around the neck | It makes you slide much too fast, so you shoot off the end and cannot stop | It gets wet and makes you cold | It hides your face from your friends
test: street lights just came on, but we're mid game | Head home now, drivers can't see you well in the dark | Finish the game first, it's fine, nobody drives fast round here at night | Stay out but wear a hoodie | Play under a street light instead
test: goal posts are wobbly, gonna swing on the crossbar | Don't, unanchored goals can fall and crush you | Fine, they're made to hold kids | Fine if you swing gently | Only if your mates hold it
test: my cousin says football means soccer in america. true? | No, in the USA it means American football | Yes, football means soccer everywhere, even in the USA and Canada | Yes, but only in schools | No, in America it means rugby
test: big hill, can i go down head first on the sled | No, sit up feet first, head first causes injuries | Yes, head first is faster and safer, coz you can see where you're going | Yes, if you wear a hat | Only on the bumpy part
explain: A boy in New Zealand swung on a portable football goal at the park; it tipped over on top of him and broke his collarbone.
explain: A girl in Florida ran across the playground with a lollipop in her mouth; she tripped, and the stick cut the back of her throat.
fact: Unanchored goals can fall on children, and some have been killed this way.
fact: A fall while holding something in the mouth can push it into the throat.
test: can i hang on the crossbar of this goal | No, goals can tip and fall on you | Yes, if it's metal | Yes, if two people hold it | Yes, if it's in a park
test: running with a lolly in my mouth | Stop and eat it standing still | Run slower | Hold the stick tighter | Keep your mouth shut
test: street lights on, can i stay out a bit more | Go home now, drivers see children less in the dark | Stay out if you wear dark clothes so cars can't see you messing about | Stay till it's pitch dark | Stay if your mates do
test: did u hear?? some kids were swinging on the football goal and "it fell right on a boy". lesson? | Never climb on or hang from a goal; tell an adult if it wobbles | Goals are too heavy to fall | Only hang from the middle of the bar | It's fine if the goal's standing on soft grass, coz then it can't hurt anyone
test: my cousin says a kid tried a backflip on the trampoline after watching videos and "can never walk again". what's the bit to trust | Don't try backflips without a coach; landing on the head can hurt the neck | Backflips are easy to learn from videos if you watch them slowly enough times first | Trampolines make every landing safe | Neck injuries only happen to adults
test: kids say the girl who sledded into the tree "can't steer, she's hopeless". pass it on? | No, that's mean; just sled feet first away from trees | Yes, she's hopeless | Yes, and laugh about it at school | Yes, it's a funny story
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct position for sledding? | Sitting up and feet first, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds | Lying head first upon the sled, for the purpose of greater speed and better steering | Standing upright upon the sled | Kneeling backwards upon the sled, so as to observe those approaching from behind
exam: Identify the principal reason for which jewellery is removed before sport. | Rings, earrings and necklaces may catch and tear skin | Jewellery slows the player down | Jewellery is liable to be stolen | Referees dislike shiny objects
exam: It is given that a child is playing outdoors at dusk. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The child shall come home when the street lights come on or at the agreed time | The child shall remain outdoors until it is fully dark, provided that friends remain also, and no adult has objected | The child shall play nearer the road for light | The child shall stay out until called by name
exam: In respect of the word «football», state which proposition is correct. | It means soccer in the UK, but American football in the USA and Canada | It means the same sport in every country | It means rugby everywhere, because both games use an oval ball and a large pitch with posts | It means cricket in Australia and New Zealand
